// B1 (adversarial): log_once called from a top-level global initializer.
//
// Risk: log_once calls __rock_log_init(), whose body checks
// `if __rock_log_level >= 0 then return end`. The Rock global
// `__rock_log_level: int = -1` provides the initial value — but Rock emits
// global initializers in declaration order at module load time. If a user's
// global initializer calls log_once BEFORE the stdlib's
// `__rock_log_level = -1` initializer runs, __rock_log_level is still
// `nil`, and `nil >= 0` raises:
//
//   attempt to compare number with nil
//
// FINDING (BUG, severity: medium):
//   log_once is NOT safe to call from a top-level global initializer.
//   Crashes at module load. Workarounds: (a) add a nil guard in
//   __rock_log_init like `if __rock_log_level == nil then __rock_log_level = -1 end`;
//   (b) ensure stdlib globals are emitted before user globals in codegen.
//   This file isolates the crash in its own module so it doesn't poison the
//   rest of the log_once test suite.
//
// This file is currently EXPECTED TO FAIL — the @test asserts the
// no-crash invariant we want, but the bug causes module load to abort.
// Leave the test as-is until the underlying bug is fixed; do not silently
// edit it to match the bad behavior.

global startup_log_count: int = {
    log_once("INIT_TEST", "from global initializer", "global-init-key")
    1
}

@test
fn test_log_once_from_global_initializer_doesnt_crash() {
    // If this @test runs at all, module load did not crash on log_once.
    assert(startup_log_count == 1)
}
